      <h3>💡 Real Estate Coach's Take</h3>
      <p>
        Here is a critical detail most buyers miss: <strong>Washington State has no mandatory "standard" testing package for private drinking wells</strong>. If you simply ask a lab to "test the well," they might only run a basic $40 bacteria swab, completely missing arsenic, heavy metals, or nitrates. Furthermore, evaluating a private well requires <strong>3 distinct steps</strong> often handled by different companies: water quality laboratory analysis, mechanical pump equipment inspection, and continuous flow rate testing. On the septic side, Snohomish County enforces a strict <strong>Report of System Status (RSS)</strong> rule before title transfer: making early contractor scheduling essential for sellers.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Educational Body Section -->
    <section class="narrative-section">
      <h2>Septic Systems: Snohomish RSS vs. King County OSM Rules</h2>
      <p>
        When buying or selling a home on a private septic system, local health districts enforce mandatory inspection and disclosure requirements prior to closing:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Snohomish County RSS Requirement:</strong> The Snohomish County Health Department requires sellers to obtain an official <strong>Report of System Status (RSS)</strong> prior to property transfer. The septic tank must be pumped and evaluated by a certified On-Site Sewage System (OSS) specialist within 12 months of closing.</li>
        <li><strong>King County OSM Standards:</strong> Public Health Seattle & King County requires routine Operation & Maintenance (O&M) inspection records. Gravity systems require inspection every 3 years, while pressure distribution or aerobic systems require annual inspections.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Private Wells: The 3-Step Evaluation Framework</h2>
      <p>
        Because private wells are unregulated by municipal utilities, buyers must conduct thorough due diligence using three separate procedures:
      </p>

      <table class="comparison-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Evaluation Step</th>
            <th>Primary Specialist</th>
            <th>What Is Tested / Verified</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>1. Water Quality Panel</strong></td>
            <td>State-Accredited Lab (PAL or AmTest)</td>
            <td>Chemical & bacteriological purity (Coliform, <em>E. coli</em>, Nitrates, Arsenic, Lead, pH).</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>2. Well Head & Pump Inspection</strong></td>
            <td>Licensed Well / Pump Contractor</td>
            <td>Sanitary surface seal, cap integrity, control box, pressure tank, and wiring safety.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>3. Flow Rate & Continuous Yield Test</strong></td>
            <td>Well Technician or Home Inspector</td>
            <td>Sustained gallon-per-minute (GPM) yield over a 1 to 4-hour continuous draw for lender underwriting.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Recommended 5-Point Water Quality Panel</h2>
      <p>
        When ordering water testing from an accredited lab such as <strong>Precision Analytical Laboratories (PAL)</strong> in Everett or <strong>AmTest Laboratories</strong> in Kirkland, explicitly request the following parameters:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Total Coliform & <em>E. coli</em> Bacteria:</strong> Checks for surface runoff or sewage contamination.</li>
        <li><strong>Nitrates & Nitrites:</strong> Identifies agricultural runoff or failing nearby septic drainfields.</li>
        <li><strong>Arsenic & Lead:</strong> Common naturally occurring heavy metals in North Sound glacial till formations.</li>
        <li><strong>Turbidity & pH Balance:</strong> Measures water clarity, suspended sediment, and pipe-corrosive acidity levels.</li>
        <li><strong>Hardness & Minerals:</strong> Evaluates iron, manganese, and mineral buildup that affects water heaters and appliances.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

